good morning I'm just delighted to join  Dean Miller and welcoming you class of  2016 to Yale College I want to welcome  also the relatives and friends who have  accompanied you here and especially your  parents as a father of four college  graduates I know how proud your parents  are you parents are of your children's  achievements and how hopeful you are for  their future and how many concerns both  large and small that you have at this  moment so let me try to reassure you  your children are going to love it here  and I expect that you are going to enjoy  your association with Yale whether you  are a returning graduate or one of the  vast majority of parents who never set  foot in new haven until your children  started to think about where to go to  college you may take comfort in learning  that surveys have shown that Yale  parents are the most satisfied in the  Ivy League  so welcome to the Yale family we're  pleased to have your children with us  and we will do our best to provide them  with abundant opportunities to learn and  thrive in the years ahead aunty you  class of 2016 welcome I suspect that you  have many different reasons for being  here you may have chosen to attend Yale  College because you heard it was a place  that attracted unusually talented and  interesting students perhaps you were  impressed by the depth and breadth of  our 2000 course offerings you may have  been drawn to the idea of residential  colleges communities that are microcosms  of the student body and that inspire  lifelong loyalties you may have learned  about the diverse array of undergraduate  organizations here devoted to politics  debate journalism and community service  organizations that will give you a  chance to develop your skills as leaders  and its collaborators you may have been  drawn by Yale's superlative  undergraduate arts organizations from  Chamber Orchestra's too dramatic  societies from dance ensembles to  acapella singing groups you may have  been recruited to one of our 35 varsity  athletic teams with their outstanding  coaches and facilities you may have  learned about our commitment to  sustainability and been interested to  join one of our environmental groups or  to work on the Yale farm or you may have  been excited by the extraordinary array  of international experiences open to you  as students in yo college all of these  are good reasons to find Yale a school  worthy of four years of your time but I  thought that I might focus this morning  on an aspect of Yale that you might not  have considered and that is the special  advantages of your having chosen to  attend a college that is situated within  one of the world's great research  universities this distinction means  first  that the faculty who will teach you here  are leading scholars in their respective  fields among the world's very best and  second that you will have access here to  virtually unmatched library and museum  resources consider first the  extraordinary collections that are  available to you in the yale university  art gallery PL center for british art  and the yale peabody museum of natural  history dozens of your professors in  literature history the history of art  anthropology geology ecology and  evolutionary biology will make use of  these museum resources in the courses  you take and perhaps some of you will  join with other students in actually  curating your own museum exhibit last  spring for undergraduates collaborated  with curators and conservators at the  Yale Center for British art to study the  materials and techniques used in the  creation of early English wood panel  paintings they created an exhibit that  was on display here at the center from  April through July a year earlier Yale  students collaborated with other  students at the University of Maryland  to create an exhibit on african-american  art drawn from Yale's collections that  was shown both here at our art gallery  and on the University of Maryland campus  now even if you don't engage quite as  deeply as to curate an exhibit I would  nonetheless urge you to visit our  museums you may discover there a love of  art or a love of nature that enriches  your entire life our libraries are an  endless source of discovery for students  who wish to engage in archival research  you'll find materials there that allow  you to undertake projects that go far  beyond what might be possible elsewhere  some years ago one undergraduate  discovered the uncataloged papers of a  deceased professor and in them he found  that a faculty organization called the  Yale library project had provided cover  for a very important World War two  intelligence mission  just this week I learned from a high  school student who was working in the  archives of the vine aki library about  his discovery of the methods used by  late 19th century railroads to finance  their sale of farmland to settlers in  the northern plains of the United States  it turns out that the railroads  strategies for helping landowners remain  on their farms during periods of  financial hardship were much more  effective than our current efforts to  protect homeowners from foreclosure  during the current recession now  consider next some examples of the  astonishingly creative and original work  of the faculty who will be teaching your  courses earlier this year professor John  Lewis gaddis professor of history won  the Pulitzer Prize for his brilliant  biography of George Kennan the diplomat  and architect of the United States cold  war strategy recently a group of  scientists led by Rick Prum a professor  of ecology and evolutionary biology was  able to deduce from fossils the colors  of feathers on a dinosaur geologist jane  wang wang has demonstrated that carbon  can be captured from the atmosphere and  sequestered not in gaseous or liquid  form beneath the earth's surface but in  solid form by catalyzing a reaction to  transform subsurface rocks into calcium  and magnesium carbonate this is one of  several technologies that Yale  scientists are pursuing in the hope of  finding solutions to the problem of  global warming historian Timothy Snyder  has shown in his recent book blood lands  the ideological and practical  connections between the nazi  extermination of the Jews and other East  Europeans and the Soviet extermination  of the same populations in her path  breaking work the ornament of the mind  professor Maria Rosa menak all  demonstrated that the Middle Ages in  Spain were  at a time of darkness and superstition  but a period in which Muslim Christian  and Jewish literature philosophy and  architecture flourished and profoundly  influenced one another in the Western  Desert of Egypt archaeologist john  darnell has unearthed a lost city a site  of a massive bread making industry more  than 3,500 years ago and finally to  complete this list of amazing research  projects your Dean Mary Miller has just  given you a glimpse a glimpse of her  immense knowledge of Mesoamerican  history art and culture in her own work  of rediscovery fee has employed infrared  photography to produce enhanced  high-resolution images of the Mayan  murals at Bonampak and she's used these  images as a platform for seminal  reinterpretations of Mayan art  architecture and civilization what's  remarkable about studying here in Yale  College is that you will have direct  access to these scholars and many others  distinguished as they are in research  they are also committed to teaching you  you will take their courses participate  in their seminars have the opportunity  to work as their research assistants or  do independent research under their  supervision most of the projects I just  described involved students in some  capacity but here are a few more in  which the role of students is front and  center consider for example professor  Scott struggles course in which  undergraduates travel over spring break  to a tropical rain forest to gather  endophytes these are microorganisms that  are found in abundance on plants the  students then return to Yale where the  work where they work for the balance of  the spring semester and throughout the  summer to characterize the organisms  they have found and discovered their  properties on recent rain forest  expedition students have found several  organisms that effectively degrade  plastic one in particular is capable of  breaking down polyurethane in the  absence of oxygen holding great promise  for practical use in the biodegradation  of buried trash or perhaps you'll be  intrigued by the opportunity to hunt for  exoplanets bodies that orbit around  stars other than our own Sun you could  do this under the supervision of  professor Deborah Fisher and her  colleagues in astronomy professor Fisher  helped to launch an online citizen  science project called planet hunters  which engages 40,000 web users in the  search for exoplanets using data  gathered from a NASA space mission three  yale college students collaborated on  the first published paper from the  planet hunter project announcing the  discovery of two previously unidentified  exoplanets two other undergraduates are  co-authors on a paper describing another  discovery that will soon be published  one of the students involved in the  first paper is now working on a team  that has developed a new device capable  of doubling the precision of the Keck  telescope making the world's largest  telescope even more powerful and finally  if you'd like to combine an interest in  the performing arts with serious study  you might consider participation in the  Yale baroque opera project conceived by  the eminent musicologist Ellen rose and  who's made major contributions to our  understanding of early music the baroque  opera project introduces students to the  historical aesthetic and performance  issues related to 19th century related  to top the Italian opera of the  seventeenth and early 18th century they  do this in their coursework and then  mount to full-scale productions each  year these examples illustrate how you  might benefit from participating in the  work of a university committed to path  breaking research I encourage you in the  strongest possible terms to take full  advantage of the people and resources  here don't be shy yells faculty  libraries and museums are here for you  if you want to get engaged with the  amazing research activities that go on  here don't hesitate to ask a professor a  librarian or a music or museum curator  they'll welcome your interest and let me  go one step farther if you want to get  the most from your Yale education you  have to be adventurous do not content  yourself with a familiar path as you  choose your courses try something  different an expository or creative  writing class statistics instead of more  calculus or new language even as you  pursue further study of one you already  know sign up for courses and projects  that will challenge you you may never  again have so much opportunity to  explore new ideas to test out new  directions to pursue different routes to  discovering your true passion stretch  yourself and I offer the same advice in  connection with the activities outside  the classrooms and museums and libraries  seek out the unfamiliar if the friends  you make here are exclusively those who  come from backgrounds just like your own  and it went to high schools just like  your own you will have forfeited half  the value of a Yale education you come  from 54 nations from a wide range of  racial religious and socioeconomic  backgrounds each of your residential  colleges contains within itself that  rich diversity seek out friends with  different histories and different  interests you will find that you learn  the most from people the least like you  in the same way as you choose your  extracurricular activities try to move  beyond the familiar try at least one  activity that is brand new to you  volunteer for community service and  begin to understand how what you have  learned here might be of value to others  work or study abroad in one of our many  summer programs and see the world from a  different perspective and so women and  men of the class of 2016 you may have  come here for many different reasons  but now that you're about to begin your  four-year journey of discovery take note  that you've come to one of the world's  great research universities then this  presents you with very special  opportunities learn from your teachers  the joy of participating in the  advancement of human understanding of  nature and culture draw upon the  abundant resources of our libraries  museums and laboratories stretch  yourselves to your limits intellectually  and interpersonally your effort will be  richly rewarded thank you
 

